  "prompt_text": "This figure illustrates how G4 DNA structures in chromatin may promote DNA opening and strand exchange during gene expression and replication. At the top, a nucleosome containing a G4 motif appears to generate a local noncanonical DNA structure that opens the surrounding chromatin fiber. This exposed configuration is then linked to two major outcomes. On the left, the open region supports transcription by recruiting factors such as TBP, Mediator, helicase, and RNA polymerase, leading to RNA synthesis. On the right, a similar opening facilitates replication, where polymerase and helicase engage the DNA and the strands are separated and copied.\n\nThe lower part presents a mechanistic model for homologous pairing or recombination driven by H-DNA–like structures. Two DNA molecules with complementary single-stranded regions seem to align and anneal, then undergo third-strand dissociation. As the duplex shifts, the strands invade matching complementary sequences, ultimately forming Holliday junctions. Overall, the figure conveys that unusual DNA secondary structures, especially G4 DNA and H-DNA, can reorganize local DNA architecture, create accessible or unstable intermediates, and thereby assist fundamental processes such as transcription, replication, and recombination.",
